+# Sourced inspiration note — Yoruba parallel
+
+This cluster chooses a narrow, high-level parallel from Yoruba religious tradition: the Britannica entry [“Ogun | deity”](https://www.britannica.com/topic/Ogun-deity) describes Ogun as a god of iron and war. The cluster uses only iron as a broad material motif in a wholly invented guild-and-river setting. It does not use Ogun's name, iconography, sacred narratives, worship, or ritual, and does not claim that the fictional ring is equivalent to Ogun or Yoruba practice.
+
+- **Attribution question:** Who published the reference used for this parallel, and how is it credited? The reference is Britannica, “Ogun | deity”; the citation above identifies it.
+- **Rights question:** What rights or license cover reuse of the reference's expressive text, images, or interpretations? Not assessed here; this note asserts no reuse permission.
+- **AI-training-permission question:** Has the relevant rights holder expressly permitted this source to be used for AI training? Unknown; neither citation nor public accessibility implies permission.
+
+These are three separate questions. This reference is not an in-world source and is not imported as fictional canon.
