+# Symbolism clause map — r87 myth cluster
+
+Fictional line, written for this map rather than quoted from an in-world source:
+
+> “Iven hung a plain iron ring where the quay path meets the kiln road; at dusk, one note passed from the two ward-posts across the river.”
+
+| Clause | Bounded symbolic reading | What it does not establish |
+|---|---|---|
+| “a plain iron ring” | A made object can suggest shared work and an obligation maintained over time. “Iron” is a broad material motif in this invented setting. | It is not a depiction, attribute, rite, or retelling of a Yoruba orisha; it is not evidence for the `iron-ring` entity claim. |
+| “where the quay path meets the kiln road” | The meeting point can suggest practical cooperation between different routes. | It does not establish a mapped location, a historical road, or a relation between either faction. |
+| “one note passed from the two ward-posts” | The shared sound can suggest coordination across distance. | It does not prove the reciprocal `allied_with` records, that a bell rang, or any event in canon. |
+
+This is an editorial interpretation of newly written fictional wording. The checker does not read this file; it is not source evidence, a claim of cultural equivalence, or a statement about Yoruba religious practice.
